<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apostolic Camera</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Camera Apostolica</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Apostolic Camera was the central financial office of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>, which from the eleventh century collected the revenues of the Holy See and, in the Papal States, administered taxes, customs, mints and state property. Headed by the cardinal camerlengo, it also acquired judicial and administrative powers, and it governs the Holy See's temporal goods during a vacancy of the papal throne.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The office of camerarius, or chamberlain, was established by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-ii">Pope Urban II</a>, and from the middle of the 12th century the papal chamberlain was a regular member of the Curia, in charge of the finances of the papal court. Its income then came chiefly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a>, dues and tributes paid by the lands subject to the pope and by churches and monasteries directly dependent on him. In 1192 Cencius Camerarius, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-honorius-iii">Pope Honorius III</a>, drew up a new inventory of these revenues, the Liber Censuum; the previous lists went back to Gelasius I and Gregory I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 13th century the Camera took on the collection and distribution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> taxes, assessed regularly after the time of Innocent III, while payment in kind gave way to payment in money. The servitia communia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and abbots were fixed at set sums, and the annates, especially after the great reservations of Clement V and John XXII, together with extraordinary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, swelled its work, which it carried out through many agents called collectores.</p><p>After the 16th century the camerlengo became one of the highest officers in the government of the Papal States, until new methods of administration at the beginning of the 19th century called for other officials. When the Papal States were annexed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in 1870, the Camera lost almost all practical influence, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>'s income came mainly from Peter's Pence, managed by a special commission.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Its highest officers were the chamberlain, regularly a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, and the treasurer, who with the clerics of the Camera, at first three or four and later up to ten, formed its governing council over revenues and expenses; below them served the auditor, notaries, scribes and messengers. Until 1870 the vice-camerlengo was governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the auditor-general presided over the supreme court. During a vacancy of the Holy See the clerics of the Camera took possession of the Apostolic palaces and took the inventories, while the vice-camerlengo kept watch over the conclave.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Apostolic Camera” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
